.\"
.\" Manual page of "mcp-python-token" - the clients of the MCP server
.\" "mcp-python", their bearer tokens.
.\"
.\" View the source: man ./src/mcp-python-token.8
.TH MCP-PYTHON-TOKEN 8 2026-11-14 "mcp-python 0.0.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
mcp-python-token \- clients of the MCP server mcp-python, their tokens
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mcp-python-token
.RB [ \-\-dir
.IR DIR ]
.B add
.I NAME
.br
.B mcp-python-token
.RB [ \-\-dir
.IR DIR ]
.B list
.br
.B mcp-python-token
.RB [ \-\-dir
.IR DIR ]
.B remove
.I NAME
.br
.B mcp-python-token
.RB { \-h | \-\-help }
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mcp-python-token
adds, lists and removes the clients of the
.BR mcp-python (8)
server. A client is a file in the directory of the token files - the file
name is the client name, the content is the SHA-256 of the token of the
client. The tool works with the files only and does not talk to the server:
a running server reads the directory again by itself, at most once per 5
seconds - no restart.
.PP
How the server uses the tokens - the header of a request, the answers, the
sessions, what a token gives - is in
.BR mcp-python (8),
AUTHENTICATION and SECURITY.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI add " NAME"
Creates the client and prints its token - random, 256 bits, with the prefix
.BR mcpp_ .
The token is alone at the standard output, the message about it is at the
standard error. It is shown this once and is not kept - it can not be shown
again, only replaced: remove the client and add it again. Fails, if the
client exists.
.TP
.B list
Prints the names of the clients, one per line. The files, which the server
skips (see FILES), are not listed.
.TP
.BI remove " NAME"
Revokes the client - its file is overwritten with zeros, then deleted.
Within 5 seconds the server refuses the token; a session of the client is
refused too, the sessions of the other clients stay.
.PP
.I NAME
\- letters, digits,
.BR . ,
.B _
and
.BR \- ,
the first one is a letter or a digit. It is a valid key of a Kubernetes
Secret.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-dir " DIR"
The directory of the token files. Default -
.B MCP_AUTH_TOKENS
of the environment, then
.IR /etc/mcp-python/tokens .
It has to exist - the tool does not create it.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Prints the usage.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MCP_AUTH_TOKENS
The directory of the token files, when there is no
.BR \-\-dir .
The same variable the server reads - but the configuration file of the
service,
.IR /etc/mcp-python/mcp-python.conf ,
is not read by the tool: with another directory set there, name it with
.BR \-\-dir .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/mcp-python/tokens
The default directory. Of the package it is of root, readable by the group
.B mcp-python
\- so
.B add
and
.B remove
need root there, and
.B list
too.
.TP
.I /etc/mcp-python/tokens/<NAME>
The client
.IR NAME ,
mode 0644, whatever the umask is - the server reads it after the privileges
drop:
.RS
.PP
.EX
# mcp-python - client 'laptop', 2026-11-14. The SHA-256 of the token.
sha256:9f86d081884c7d659a2feaa0c55ad015a3bf4f1b2b0b822cd15d6c15b0f00a08
.EE
.PP
Read is the first line, which is not empty and not a comment
.RB ( # ).
The file does not let anyone in - a token can not be derived from it; so it
is safe to read, to copy and to mount. A file made by another means is
accepted too, if it has such a line - but a token chosen by a human is
guessable, and then the hash is not a protection.
.PP
The server and
.B list
skip the entries with a leading dot (a file being written by
.BR add ,
.I ..data
of a Kubernetes Secret volume), the directories, the files with a name not
valid for a client and the files without a
.B sha256:
line. The server reports a skipped file in its log.
.RE
.TP
.I /usr/lib/mcp-python/mcp_auth.py
The tool itself -
.I /usr/sbin/mcp-python-token
runs it with
.BR python3 .
Standard library only - no virtual environment is needed.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Done.
.TP
.B 1
A failure, with a message at the standard error - the client exists
.RB ( add ),
no such client
.RB ( remove ),
a wrong name, the directory is missing or there are no rights to it.
.TP
.B 2
A wrong command line.
.SH NOTES
.B remove
overwrites a file in place, as
.BR shred (1)
does. Whether the old content is really gone is up to the file system and
the disk - it is not guaranteed with a copy-on-write file system or an SSD.
The file holds a hash, not a token - it is a second line of defence.
.PP
A token is a password: who has it, executes a code at the server. Keep it
where the client keeps its secrets; do not pass it to the server side
through the environment - see
.BR mcp-python (8),
SECURITY.
.PP
Where the package is not installed - a container host, a build machine -
the tool runs from the source tree:
.BR "python3 src/mcp_auth.py" ,
with the same arguments.
.SH EXAMPLES
Add a client, keep its token in a file only the caller reads, see the
clients, revoke it:
.PP
.RS
.EX
(umask 077 ; sudo mcp-python-token add laptop > laptop.token)
sudo mcp-python-token list
sudo mcp-python-token remove laptop
.EE
.RE
.PP
Replace the token of a client - the old one stops working:
.PP
.RS
.EX
sudo mcp-python-token remove laptop
sudo mcp-python-token add laptop
.EE
.RE
.PP
The clients of a container - a directory at the host
.RI ( <tokens-dir> ),
mounted read only at
.I /etc/mcp-python/tokens
of the container:
.PP
.RS
.EX
mkdir <tokens-dir>
python3 src/mcp_auth.py \-\-dir <tokens-dir> add laptop
.EE
.RE
.PP
The clients of a Kubernetes deployment - the same directory as a Secret, a
key per client
.RB ( auth.secretName
of the Helm chart). A client added or revoked - apply the Secret again:
.PP
.RS
.EX
kubectl create secret generic <secret-name> \e
    \-\-from-file=<tokens-dir> \e
    \-\-dry-run=client \-\-output=yaml | kubectl apply \-\-filename=\-
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mcp-python (8),
.BR shred (1),
.BR sha256sum (1)
